From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] common: mount a private tmpfs on /run in chroot_setup

chroot_setup, used by pacstrap and by arch-chroot without -S or -N,
bind-mounts the host's /run into the new root. pacman's hooks run
chrooted in it, and systemd's 21-systemd-tmpfiles.hook runs
`systemd-tmpfiles --create` there. That resolves user names in the new
root's /etc/passwd, and its `d` lines also adjust the owner of
directories that already exist, so it gives the host's /run/systemd/netif
(and links/, leases/) to the new root's systemd-network uid. The host's
systemd-networkd then can no longer write its state files:

  systemd-networkd: Failed to update state file /run/systemd/netif/state, ignoring: Permission denied
  ve-arch: Failed to update link state file /run/systemd/netif/links/5, ignoring: Permission denied

It goes unnoticed only when the host numbers its users as the new root
does, which a host other than Arch Linux, such as Cherry, does not.

Give the new root a private /run instead, as unshare_setup (-N) already
does, and as arch-chroot -S does through MountAPIVFS=yes.

Upstream: not submitted
Signed-off-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 common | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/common b/common
--- a/common
+++ b/common
@@ -57,7 +57,7 @@
   chroot_add_mount udev "$1/dev" -t devtmpfs -o mode=0755,nosuid &&
   chroot_add_mount devpts "$1/dev/pts" -t devpts -o mode=0620,gid=5,nosuid,noexec &&
   chroot_add_mount shm "$1/dev/shm" -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,nosuid,nodev &&
-  chroot_add_mount /run "$1/run" --bind --make-private &&
+  chroot_add_mount run "$1/run" -t tmpfs -o nosuid,nodev,mode=0755 &&
   chroot_add_mount tmp "$1/tmp" -t tmpfs -o mode=1777,strictatime,nodev,nosuid
 }
 
